//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// What the model says about host access to a space travels to the backend.
//
// `managed:` decides whether the host may read a space, and the checker has
// always honoured it. The runtime never saw it: `vx_plugin_alloc_and_transfer`
// took only a size, a source and a topology, so a space declared host-readable
// was staged with cudaMalloc anyway -- host-unreadable memory the checker had
// been told the host could read. That gap is not observable until something
// performs the host read, which is a SIGSEGV in the memref printer on a machine
// with a device, and nothing at all on a machine without one.
//
// So the claim is carried down as a fourth argument and the backend that knows
// whether a device is really present compares the two. The values are
// `vx_space_access` in include/vx_hardware_runtime.h: 1 for a space the program
// must transfer in and out of explicitly, 2 for one the program believes the
// host can read.
//
// Both appear here, which is what makes this worth pinning. The staging
// transfer targets GPU_HBM, declared `managed: explicit`, and carries 1. The
// transfer home targets the host and carries 2. A renumbering of the enum, or a
// lowering that stopped reading the attribute and passed a constant, moves one
// of these and not the other.
//
// Checked after translation to LLVM IR, and that choice is the whole reason
// this fixture is sound. At the LLVM *dialect* stage a constant is a separate
// SSA definition, so `llvm.mlir.constant(2 : i32)` is satisfied by any
// incidental 2 -- and a program with tensors and loops emits them for strides,
// indices and bounds. The first version of this file checked exactly that and
// stayed green under a lowering forced to emit a constant, because the check had
// bound itself to an unrelated stride. Translated, the values are immediate
// operands of the call, so one line carries the callee, the topology and the
// mode together and there is nothing for an incidental constant to satisfy.
// This is the first executable RUN line in the suite to use `mlir-translate`
// -- ane_conv.vx and its neighbours mention it only inside commented-out
// pipelines -- so it names the tool the way the project already learned to.
// `MLIR_TRANSLATE_PATH` is what CI sets (to `mlir-translate-22`), because
// resolving these tools by bare name is what Vx#496 is about; the fallback
// keeps a plain checkout working. The RUN line goes through
// `bash -c "set -o pipefail; ..."`, so the expansion applies and a missing tool
// fails the line rather than feeding FileCheck an empty stream it would pass on.
// There is no generic skip: the runner honours only `// REQUIRES: macos`, so on
// a box without the tool this reddens rather than skipping, and the fault is
// the environment rather than the compiler.
//
// Each line below therefore fails on its own account: forcing the lowering to
// emit 2 unconditionally leaves the first unmatched, forcing 1 leaves the
// second unmatched. The topology ids also tell the two calls apart without
// relying on their order -- 500 is the GPU topology, 0 the host.
//
// One trap worth naming, because its answer looks wrong rather than broken: a
// program that never transfers its result home is refused before emission, by
// the E6003 this argument is the backstop for. Reaching for this check on a
// program of that shape produces no matching lines at all, which reads as "the
// value is not emitted" instead of "this program was rejected". Hence the
// transfer home below.
//
// And the RUN line below is not run as written. Files in THIS directory -- and
// only this one -- get `--legacy-codegen` injected, because these fixtures pin
// the AST codegen's MLIR structure (`run_optimization_test`, which is the sole
// caller passing force_legacy; the backend and frontend runners pass false). So
// what is checked here is the AST path, and `// REQUIRES: flat-codegen` is the
// opt-out. A fixture elsewhere in tests/ is not overridden and should not pin a
// path defensively as though it were.
//
// Which makes one wildcard above load-bearing in a way worth stating. The size
// operand is an immediate on the default path, `i64 16`, and an SSA value on the
// injected one, `i64 %20`. Written as the literal it plainly is, this fixture
// would be green locally and red in CI. `i64 {{.*}}` spans both -- but a
// wildcard that absorbs a real difference between paths looks exactly like one
// that is merely tolerant, and which it is only becomes visible when the paths
// diverge somewhere nobody was looking. The two operands this file is actually
// about are literals for that reason.
//
// RUN: vxc %s --action emit-llvm 2>/dev/null | ${MLIR_TRANSLATE_PATH:-mlir-translate} --mlir-to-llvmir | FileCheck %s
//
// CHECK: declare ptr @vx_plugin_alloc_and_transfer(i64, ptr, i32, i32)
//
// Staging onto the device: `managed: explicit`, so 1.
// CHECK: call ptr @vx_plugin_alloc_and_transfer(i64 {{.*}}, ptr {{.*}}, i32 500, i32 1)
//
// And home again, to a space the host can read, so 2.
// CHECK: call ptr @vx_plugin_alloc_and_transfer(i64 {{.*}}, ptr {{.*}}, i32 0, i32 2)
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//

Memory CPU_DRAM {}
Memory GPU_HBM {
  within: Memory::CPU_DRAM, capacity: 40 GiB, bandwidth: 3 TB/s, managed: explicit
}

fn main() -> i32 {
  let mut a_h = Tensor<f32, [2, 2]>::uninit();
  for i in 0..2 {
    for j in 0..2 {
      a_h[i][j] = 1.0;
    }
  }
  // Into the device space: the program is obliged to move data in and out, so
  // device memory is what it expects.
  let a = transfer(a_h, Memory::GPU_HBM);
  // And back, so `print` reads a space the host can actually read. Without this
  // the program is refused before it reaches emission, which is the compile-time
  // check this argument is the backstop for.
  let home = transfer(a, Memory::CPU_DRAM);
  print(home);
  return 0;
}
